# Python File Size Rule

## Hard Limit

Every Python file in this repo MUST be **≤ 300 lines** (including imports, docstrings, and blank lines). This is a hard limit, not a guideline.

## Scope

- Applies to every `**/*.py` file touched by the agent from Phase 0.1 onward.
- Existing files over 300 lines are **not retroactively broken**, but any file the agent edits must be brought into compliance in the same change.
- New files must be born compliant.

## How to Split

If an implementation needs more than 300 lines, split it into a package under `src/atoms_vs_ashes/<area>/` with **one responsibility per module** and a thin orchestrator that imports the pieces.

Follow the existing modular patterns:

- `src/atoms_vs_ashes/analysis/` — one criterion per file (`aviation_hazard.py`, `epz_population.py`, `land_availability.py`, …); the package `__init__.py` re-exports the public API.
- `src/atoms_vs_ashes/screening/` — `base.py` for shared primitives, one file per screening dimension (`grid_capacity.py`, `land_area.py`, …).

## Examples

```
# BAD — one 800-line module
src/atoms_vs_ashes/scoring.py            # 800 lines

# GOOD — package with one responsibility per module
src/atoms_vs_ashes/scoring/
    __init__.py          # re-exports public API
    engine.py            # orchestrator, ≤ 300 lines
    rubric.py            # YAML loader + schema
    bands.py             # value → (score, low, high)
    exclusionary.py      # E1–E9 evaluators
    avoidance.py         # A1–A15 evaluators
    composite.py         # Σ wᵢ·cᵢ
    merge_resolver.py    # API > LLM > default
    sensitivity.py       # weight / MC / threshold perturbations
```

## Enforcement

- Before finishing a task, count lines on every Python file you touched (`wc -l <file>`); if any is > 300, split it before marking the task done.
- If splitting is genuinely impossible (e.g. an auto-generated Alembic migration), call it out explicitly in the commit message so a human can review.
